test_timing -- del_vor's time and memory against size, and against LaGriT (Nov 2026)
=======================================================================

For development: not run by the default run_all.sh (it needs LaGriT
and big meshes, and takes minutes). Run it alone or with
ONLY=timing sh run_all.sh.

QUICK START

  LAGRIT=/path/to/lagrit sh run_timing_tests.sh [path-to-del_vor]
  open summary.html

  The first run makes the meshes with LaGriT (make_timing_meshes.sh)
  in DEL_VOR_BIG/timing (default ~/lagrit_work/del_vor_dev/big_inputs,
  outside git); later runs reuse them. Without LAGRIT, only del_vor is
  timed, on meshes already there. With the default sizes: about 3
  minutes on Claude's sandbox.

  Bigger sizes (Carl's machine), for example up to 30 million tets:

  SIZES_TET="100000 300000 1000000 3000000 10000000 30000000" \
  SIZES_TRI="100000 1000000 10000000" \
  LAGRIT=/path/to/lagrit sh run_timing_tests.sh

  then send logs/timing.csv back. Each mesh is made once (a 10
  million tet .inp is about 0.7 GB); each measurement is the best of
  REPEAT runs (default 3; REPEAT=1 for the biggest sizes saves time).

WHAT IS TESTED

  tet_N, tri_N   jittered grids of about N tets (triangles), made by
                 LaGriT: createpts, perturb of the interior vertices
                 by a fifth of the spacing, connect, dump / avs
  dfn_*          any fracture network put in DEL_VOR_BIG/timing as
                 dfn_NAME.inp (dfnWorks full_mesh.inp files)

  del_vor -avs mesh.inp -stor mesh.stor -delaunay_tol 1 -timing on
    timing on prints the wall and CPU time of each step and the peak
    memory (development only; off by default). delaunay_tol 1 because
    LaGriT's connect leaves a few facets that are not Delaunay on
    these meshes (see below); the check still runs and is timed.
  LaGriT, twice: read / avs alone, and read / avs + dump / stor;
    dump / stor's time is the difference.

WHAT IS CHECKED (timing.py)

  slope_total     the slope of log(del_vor's total time) against
                  log(cells), over the sizes taking at least 0.05 s:
                  1 is linear. PASS when <= 1.2 (MEASURED when the
                  sizes are too few or too small to tell)
  slope STEP      the same for every step that takes 5 % or more of
                  the total. MEASURED
  not_delaunay    facets with delta/L < -1e-10 (LaGriT's connect
                  leaves a few). MEASURED
  memory          peak memory per cell. MEASURED
  vs_lagrit       del_vor against LaGriT at the largest size. MEASURED

MEASURED ON CLAUDE'S SANDBOX (Nov 10, 2026; 2 cores)

  1,019,162 tets: del_vor 4.9 s, 266 MB; LaGriT read + dump / stor
  13.0 s (dump / stor alone 10.6 s; del_vor's steps that do that
  work, 2.2 s), 467 MB. Slopes: del_vor 1.02 to 1.07 for every step;
  LaGriT's dump / stor 1.18.
  996,872 triangles: del_vor 3.7 s, 304 MB; LaGriT 8.2 s, 912 MB.
  LaGriT's connect left 15 facets with delta/L < -1e-10 in the
  million-tet mesh (smallest -3.6e-4, confirmed in exact arithmetic
  by ../exact_check.py).

